 'seed_insight_business_led_2','seed_insight_group_wordpress_vs_custom_website_which_is_right_for_you','en',
 $q$WordPress vs Custom Website: Which Is Right for Your Business?$q$,
 $q$wordpress-vs-custom-website$q$,
 $q$Compare WordPress and custom website development by content needs, integrations, ownership, performance, security, budget, and long-term change.$q$,
 $json$[{"type":"paragraph","text":"The practical question in wordpress vs custom website at a glance is not whether an option is possible. It is whether it solves the intended business problem with an acceptable level of cost, complexity, risk, and future change. W3Techs reports WordPress remains the most widely used CMS on the web, making its ecosystem and maintenance model relevant to many business sites."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Teams often make this decision too early. Start by defining the audience, the job the website must perform, and the constraints that could make one approach unsuitable. WordPress is often a strong fit when teams need frequent publishing, familiar editorial workflows, and a broad extension ecosystem."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is why a visible deliverable can be misleading. Two websites can look similar while having very different content models, integrations, ownership requirements, and operating costs. Custom development becomes more defensible when the website must implement distinctive business rules, complex integrations, specialized workflows, or product-like behavior."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A useful evaluation connects the decision to evidence: requirements, user journeys, content needs, technical constraints, and what the team must be able to change after launch. A custom stack still needs content management, security, testing, deployment, monitoring, and a team capable of changing it safely."},{"type":"callout","tone":"info","title":"The principle","text":"W3Techs reports WordPress remains the most widely used CMS on the web, making its ecosystem and maintenance model relevant to many business sites."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"WordPress vs Custom Website at a Glance"},{"type":"table","headers":["Decision area","What to evaluate","Why it matters"],"rows":[["WordPress is a real platform, not a shortcut","W3Techs reports WordPress remains the most widely used CMS on the web, making its ecosystem and maintenance model relevant to many business sites.","Changes the initial decision"],["Custom does not automatically mean better","WordPress is often a strong fit when teams need frequent publishing, familiar editorial workflows, and a broad extension ecosystem.","Changes future effort"],["Start with the operating model","Custom development becomes more defensible when the website must implement distinctive business rules, complex integrations, specialized workflows, or product-like behavior.","Changes the initial decision"],["Plugins can accelerate delivery","A custom stack still needs content management, security, testing, deployment, monitoring, and a team capable of changing it safely.","Changes future effort"],["CMS ownership matters","Performance is not determined by the platform label alone; themes, plugins, assets, rendering, hosting, caching, and implementation all matter.","Changes the initial decision"]],"caption":"A practical decision model for WordPress vs Custom Website: Which Is Right for Your Business?."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/wordpress-vs-custom-website/hero.svg","alt":"Editorial visual: WordPress vs Custom Website at a Glance","caption":"A visual model for wordpress vs custom website at a glance."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The goal is not to maximize features. The goal is to create the smallest credible system that supports the business outcome and can be operated safely. Performance is not determined by the platform label alone; themes, plugins, assets, rendering, hosting, caching, and implementation all matter."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What WordPress Does Well"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Cost and quality should be considered together. A cheap decision can become expensive when missing discovery, QA, content work, or maintenance appears later. WordPress documentation emphasizes keeping core, themes, and plugins updated and maintaining reliable backups."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"WordPress is a real platform, no